<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Champa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Champa was a group of kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> people on the central and southern coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, from the early centuries CE until absorbed by Vietnam. It was a maritime trading power linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Fast-ripening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> varieties from Champa transformed agriculture under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/champa.jpg" alt="Champa" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laska666 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linyi_and_Chamic_states_of_the_fifth_century_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Champa was a collection of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> polities along the coast of what is now central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, from about the 2nd century CE until 1832. According to the earliest sources the first Cham polities arose in the 2nd and 3rd centuries, after Khu Liên's rebellion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a>. They grew out of the seafaring Austronesian Sa Huỳnh culture. Scholars disagree about how far Champa was ever united: older writers saw one kingdom, while others argue that it was mostly a confederation of kingdoms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Wars with the neighbouring kingdom of Funan in the 4th century brought Indian culture into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> society, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> became the state religion. The Đông Yên Châu inscription, of about 350, is the oldest known text in a native Southeast Asian language. King Vikrantavarman I, who reigned from 653 to 686, had many sanctuaries built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-son">Mỹ Sơn</a> and sent regular embassies to the Tang court. Champa reached its height in the 9th and 10th centuries, and its trade linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> until the 17th century.</p><p>In 1471 Champa suffered serious defeats by the Vietnamese state of Đại Việt, in which 120,000 people were captured or killed, among them the king, Tra Toan, who died in captivity on the way north. What remained was a small enclave near Nha Trang and Phan Rang, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Chams</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. In 1832 the emperor Minh Mạng annexed the last Cham territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> began to spread among the Chams after the 10th century, and by the 17th century the royal families had adopted it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a>, Rade and Jarai peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> speak Chamic languages, and many Cham today are Muslim, while the Bacam keep a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. Cham red brick temples survive in many places. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-son">Mỹ Sơn</a>, a former religious centre, and Hội An are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a>, and the largest collection of Cham sculpture is in the museum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-nang">Da Nang</a>, founded in 1915.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Champa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name outlived the polity: Zhou Daguan still used it in the 13th century, it stands on the Mao Kun map, and after Cambodia overran <b>Champa</b> in 1195–1200 the Chinese renamed the country Zhanla, &quot;Defeated Chams&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Funan also fought on the Chinese frontier: in the 270s it joined its neighbour Linyi, the region of <b>Champa</b>, to attack Tongking in northern Vietnam, then a Chinese colony.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funan">Funan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Expansion reached its height under rulers such as Suryavarman II, who campaigned against Dai Viet and <b>Champa</b>, and Jayavarman VII.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Bimbisara annexed the eastern kingdom of Anga, with its river port of <b>Champa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rabatak inscription claims that Kanishka's authority reached cities such as Saketa, Kausambi, Pataliputra and <b>Champa</b>, though how far this meant direct rule rather than recognition is uncertain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1471 he led a great expedition against <b>Champa</b>, captured its capital Vijaya and its king, and annexed the northern Cham lands, leaving a much reduced Champa in the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
